 
          <p>At the end of 2024, I made <a href="/post/2024/11/i-made-my-own-top-100-page/">my own video game Top 100 page</a>, resulting in a cool grid-based list available at <a href="https://jefklakscodex.com/top100/">https://jefklakscodex.com/top100/</a>. While writing <a href="/post/2026/10/a-huge-pile-called-the-ongoing-backlog/">A Huge Pile Called The Ongoing Backlog</a>, I discovered even more websites dedicated to categorising and indexing your backlog: Giant Bomb&rsquo;s Video Game Tracker, Backloggery, Backloggd, MyVideoGameList, Completionator, Darkadia, Searchtoplay, &hellip; Every few months a new system seems to pop up&mdash;and go permanently down, taking your data with it. You know where I&rsquo;m going with this&hellip;</p>
<p>So yes, I created my own backlog based on the Top 100 grid system, now deployed at <a href="https://jefklakscodex.com/backlog/">https://jefklakscodex.com/backlog/</a>. I already had all the data living in separate Markdown so all I had to do is add some more entries and add a few backlog-specific bits and pieces. The result doesn&rsquo;t look too shabby:</p>
<p>
<figure><a href="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/creating-your-own-gaming-backlog-yields-behavioural-insights/backlog.jpg" class="lbox">
		<img src="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/creating-your-own-gaming-backlog-yields-behavioural-insights/backlog.jpg" loading="lazy" title="Jefklak&#39;s Codex Backlog, split between currently playing and the rest of the backlog." data-pagefind-index-attrs="title">
	</a>
	
		<figcaption>Jefklak&#39;s Codex Backlog, split between currently playing and the rest of the backlog.</figcaption></figure>
</p>
<p>The technical design is fairly simple: it&rsquo;s just a Hugo <code>{{ range }}</code> with some filters, fetching data from Markdown frontmatter. An example of a file looks like this:</p>
<pre tabindex="0"><code>---
title: &#34;Ball x Pit&#34;
ignore: true
backlog: &#34;backlog&#34;
purchase_date: 2026-09-25
howlongtobeat_id: 169298
howlongtobeat_hrs: 19.2
game_name: &#34;Ball x Pit&#34;
game_genre: &#34;puzzle&#34;
game_release_year: 2025
game_developer: &#34;Kenny Sun&#34;
tags:
  - &#34;puzzle&#34;
  - &#34;roguelike&#34;
---
</code></pre><p>Where the How Long To Beat (HLTB) data and the cover image is automatically fetched through an external script. The <code>backlog</code> key is relevant here, as is <code>ignore</code> to keep it from popping up on the main site including the RSS feed before finishing it and writing down my thoughts. See my <a href="/post/2021/07/reducing-workflow-load-facilitates-writing/">blog workflow post</a> for more technical details.</p>
<p>A few of the features were ported back to the Top 100 page, such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>Filtering by physical games;</li>
<li>Filtering on average game length (in buckets: 0-5 hours, 5-10 hours, &hellip;);</li>
<li>Activating multiple filters at once;</li>
<li>The enhanced sort combobox.</li>
</ul>
<p>The idea for using average game length buckets was to mimic the Nintendo Switch&rsquo;s way of recording playtime. That way, when I&rsquo;m in the mood for a shorter game, I can quickly glance over the backlog and filter out the ones that&rsquo;ll take me too long to finish.</p>
<p>As part of the <strong>Backlog Statistics</strong>,  a new graph called &ldquo;Average Game Length&rdquo; was added to visualise the game length distribution in the backlog. Both the Top 100 and the Backlog pages showcase the same pattern: most of the games take beetween 5 and 10 hours; then between 10 and 20; then between 0 and 5 and between 20 and 30; with the fewest between 30 and 40 and still too many games breaking through the 40+ hour mark. Unsurprisingly, the three <em>Dragon Quest</em> games lying on the shelf collecting dust are responsible for that. You can also click on one of the bars in the graph which will automatically filter on that game length.</p>
<p>
<figure><a href="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/creating-your-own-gaming-backlog-yields-behavioural-insights/stats.jpg" class="lbox">
		<img src="https://brainbaking.com/post/2026/11/creating-your-own-gaming-backlog-yields-behavioural-insights/stats.jpg" loading="lazy" title="A part of the backlog stats: games by release year and platform." data-pagefind-index-attrs="title">
	</a>
	
		<figcaption>A part of the backlog stats: games by release year and platform.</figcaption></figure>
</p>
<p>Fifty-eight of the games on the backlog are <strong>physical versions</strong>, with the remaining twenty-two being digital releases. I tried being honest by including every possible game I have yet to play, including the Switch eShop, Steam, and GOG.com library. I stopped there: the SNES Mini and PSX Classic also house twenty-something titles that I have yet to properly discover<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>All games are legally-owned. I have zero intentions of downloading yet another ROM library that would completely flood the backlog. At this moment, I am playing 10 games, and have 70 more waiting for me. In other words, <strong>I&rsquo;m drowning in games.</strong> I&rsquo;ve said it before and will say it again: <a href="/post/2025/07/too-many-games-too-little-time/">Too Many Games, Too Little Time</a>. The amount of hours required to beat the entire backlog is <code>1421</code> hours which would require almost two full years of playing two hours a day every single day. Given other obligations, this is already a very optimistic calculation. My <a href="/post/2025/12/2025-in-video-games/">gaming habits yearnotes</a> tell me I finished 27 games in 2025 and 2024: that means it&rsquo;ll take three instead of two years to finish all 80 currently on the backlog. Also, a large backlog brings up another problem: is there any time left to replay the classics?</p>
<p>Another interesting statistic: when looking at the games by release year, you can see that most games are relatively recent compared to the same stats from the Top 100. This is not a big surprise: the last few years, I&rsquo;ve been buying mostly (physical) Switch games&mdash;hence the dominating group of 28 games from that platform, with the PC following suit with 22 games. It&rsquo;s been awfully quiet when it comes to scoring good deals at GB(C) games on flea markets. The ridiculous prices demanded by the second hand cartridge market for once works in my favour.</p>
<p>I went to great lengths to try and add correct <strong>purchase dates</strong>&mdash;the default sort property. This means I can now see how many games I bought this year, last year, and the year before. Going further back, things became increasingly difficult to pinpoint. Let&rsquo;s examine 2025 and 2026 a bit more closely.</p>
<ul>
<li>Bought in 2026: 20 in total (8 physical, 12 digital). Played: 12. Backlog: 8. Physical &amp; played: 0. Digital &amp; played: 12.</li>
<li>Bought in 2025: 19 in total (12 physical, 7 digital). Played: 9. Backlog: 10. Physical &amp; played: 5. Digital &amp; played: 4.</li>
</ul>
<p>This lays bare a painful truth about my video game consumerism: I buy a lot of physical games to place them on the shelf. Especially this year, exactly zero of those purchased within the same year were finished. And yet every single digital game I bought was played &amp; reviewed on The Codex. Why?</p>
<p>Here are couple of possible explanations. First, I am suffering from a well-known condition called Fear Of Missing Out when it comes to physical (and very limited) Switch releases. So whenever I see one that&rsquo;s been on my radar of a while or is priced somewhat right, I can&rsquo;t resist. Also, Joel puts me up to it. Second, the digital games I buy are sometimes influenced by others I follow or recent releases I want to play <em>now</em>. Third, many of these are indie games that will never get a physical release. Fourth, when I order a physical game, it takes a while to get here. By the time they&rsquo;re here, another game has been queued up and it has to wait.</p>
<p>I was wrong <a href="/post/2025/11/is-collecting-physical-games-worth-it-part-iv/">about collecting physical games</a>. It looks like I&rsquo;ve become a hoarder and not a player. The harsh truth here is that I could be happier by going fully digital and <em>only</em> buying when I feel like playing a particular game which greatly increases the chances of said game being played and finished. Another observation: I am immune to &ldquo;deals&rdquo; and price fluctuations for digital games. I ignore Steam sales, Humble Bundle et al., and I buy a game (usually through <code>gog.com</code> or <code>itch.io</code>) regardless of discount. I buy because I want to play, yet I am worrying about prices and ordering too late when it comes to physical releases. Who&rsquo;s being stupid now?</p>
<hr>
<p>So what now? Good question. This exercise hasn&rsquo;t exactly left me roaring to go and discover even more hidden gems. The more things being piled up on the backlog, the more depressed I become, yet I keep wanting to buy these limited runs because &ldquo;I have to, this is my only shot&rdquo;.</p>
<p>I know that compared to others, my backlog is relatively small. My definition of &ldquo;backlog&rdquo; is anything I purchased, including the dreading Steam library that in my case is pretty manageable. Yet something&rsquo;s gotta give.</p>
<p>Here&rsquo;s a promise to myself for 2027: I will <strong>not buy a single new video game</strong>. Not one. Zip. Nada. I will instead try to reduce the backlog and will be extremely happy when at the end of next year there are 50 games on there instead of 80. The ones I want to buy will firmly stay on the wishlist. Perhaps I should also be very selective when it comes to adding them there, otherwise for 2028 the wishlist will explode and the buying spree will simply come back.</p>
<p>If I just focus on the games in HLTB bucket 0-5 and 5-10, that&rsquo;ll be 37 ones scrapped off. I could force myself to power through a few longer games, but I know I&rsquo;ll just be dropping it halfway through. The longest games I managed to tackle the last two years were about 30 hours. I will also permit myself to trim the backlog by selling games if I have to (which is of course only possible with physical releases).</p>
<p>My biggest concern with this challenge is, again, the Limited Run Games et al. releases. I will never completely miss out on these games: there&rsquo;s almost always the option to buy them digitally. Perhaps by the end of 2027, I&rsquo;ll become a digital convert, who knows!</p>
<p>Wish me luck! I&rsquo;ll be needing it&hellip;</p>
